# Edge mode, choice (i): Cloudflare Origin CA certificate.
#
# When DNS sits behind Cloudflare with the orange cloud on, ACME cannot
# reach your origin — the edge answers the HTTP-01 challenge for a hostname
# that no longer resolves to you. The fix people reach for first is to turn
# SSL/TLS off or set it to Flexible. Do not: Flexible means the browser sees
# https and the edge talks to your origin in plaintext over the public
# internet, which is a downgrade any observer on the path can replay.
#
# The right answer is a Cloudflare Origin CA certificate: Cloudflare signs it
# as its own CA, it is valid only between the edge and your origin, it needs
# no ACME and no inbound port 80, and it pairs with SSL/TLS mode **Full
# (strict)** so the edge validates the certificate rather than accepting
# anything. Cloudflare renews it on its own schedule — you re-issue from the
# dashboard every 15 years, which is the long expiry it ships with.
#
# Use choice (ii) (Caddyfile.cf-dns01) instead when a publicly trusted
# certificate is required at the origin — for example a second proxy or a
# monitoring agent that refuses a private CA, or an origin you want reachable
# directly without the edge in the path.
#
# Required env:
#   MARULA_DOMAIN     the public hostname
# Files (mounted read-only at /etc/caddy/certs):
#   origin.crt        the certificate Cloudflare issued (PEM, with the leaf
#                     first — Caddy accepts the full chain in one file)
#   origin.key        the matching private key (PEM)
# Optional:
#   MARULA_MAX_UPLOAD_MB  must match core-api's value (default 25).
{
	# No ACME section at all: this mode never talks to a public CA.
	# Pinned to IPv4 loopback on purpose: busybox wget resolves
	# `localhost` to ::1 first, and a healthcheck that cannot connect is a
	# container that reports unhealthy forever.
	# IPv4 loopback, pinned: busybox wget resolves `localhost` to ::1
	# first, so a `localhost` admin address makes the container's own
	# healthcheck fail forever.
	admin 127.0.0.1:2019

	# Port 80 still gets the https redirect for anything that arrives over
	# plaintext (a bookmarked URL, a bot that ignores the redirect). It is
	# not a challenge endpoint in this mode; you can close 80 at the host
	# firewall and keep the redirect as belt-and-braces for the local
	# network.
}

https://{$MARULA_DOMAIN:marula.localhost} {
	import marula-routes.caddy

	# Caddy serves these files and never asks a CA for anything. Cloudflare
	# validates them against its own root, which is what Full (strict)
	# means. The certificate's SAN has to list MARULA_DOMAIN exactly — a
	# wildcard *.example.com covers marula.example.com, a dedicated
	# origin certificate for the host covers only that host.
	tls {$CADDY_CERTS_DIR:/etc/caddy/certs}/origin.crt {$CADDY_CERTS_DIR:/etc/caddy/certs}/origin.key
}
